                                  TM* HELP
===============================================================================
To print this help file: in DOS, type "PRINT TM.HLP"
(Your printout will contain many "%" and "!"; they are just markers to
 highlight specific characters.)
===============================================================================

Each of the following examples describes procedures for a particular 
basic task.  You need not follow them to the letter; they only show some
typical uses of TM*.  (Each highlighted letter corresponds to the key you
must press to select an option.  Refer to the summaries of MAIN-MENU and
OPTIONS-MENU that appear below the examples for explanations.)

To enter a new program and run it:
   (a) Select %!Edit/Create option from MAIN-MENU.
   (b) Enter and edit your program.
   (c) From MAIN-MENU, select %!Save to disk.  (This step is recommended to
       preserve the table that you created in case it is later modified or
       lost.)
   (d) If you wish to change the default initial TM state (= 1), then from
       MAIN-MENU, select %!Current State.
   (e) If you wish to load an input tape, then select %!Load from the "Tape"
       column of MAIN-MENU.
   (f) From MAIN-MENU select %!Run; then select %!Continuous or %!Step-by-step
       to test your program.
   (g) If you are not satisfied with the results, then either select
       %!EditTable or return to MAIN-MENU and select %!Edit/Create; then if
       you wish to start the program from the beginning, select %!Initialize
       from MAIN-MENU and return to step (f).
   (h) When you are finished or just want to quit:  remember to SAVE your
       program before you leave TM* if you do not wish to lose it forever!

To load a previously saved program and run it:
   (a) At MAIN-MENU the stored TM program files are listed.  Select %!Load from
       the "Program" column.
   (b) Go to step (d) above.
   [ Suggestion: Try loading and running BUSY2.
     (See comments, explanations by setting %!Display to Scroller and
      invoking %!EditTable.) ]

To run UTM (Universal Turing Machine):
   (a) From MAIN-MENU select %!UTM mode.
   (b) If you want to run a stored UTM input tape, select %!Load under the Tape
       column.  (Generally, input tapes for the UTM have names beginning with
       "UTM..." displayed in MAIN-MENU file directory area.)
   (c) From MAIN-MENU select %!Run.  (Select UTM.HLP if you wish.)


                             SUMMARY OF MAIN-MENU
                             --------------------
 For TM program:
   %!Load from disk:
      Load a program stored on the disk (and displayed in the file directory),
      initializing the current state to the initial state specified for the
      program and clearing the initial tape.
   %!Edit/Create:
      The edit screen has six labeled columns.  The entries under the first
      column labled CST for "current state" are permanently set and cannot be
      altered.  You can enter and edit the entries under the other five columns
      with the following restrictions:

         INP for "input"      - any symbol you can type
         OUT for "output"     - any symbol you can type
         MOV for "head move"  - only "L", "R", or "N"
         NST for "next state" - a number between 0 and 99  [ 0 = HALT ]
         Comments             - explanation and description of program

      (Under INP and OUT, the "?" symbol has a special meaning.  For INP, it
       specifies all input symbol(s) other than the ones that already appear
       above and below within the same state.  For OUT, it will mean that the
       same symbol that is already on the cell of the tape in which the
       read/write head is currently pointing to will be output, or
       equivalently, it will mean that there is no output.)

      The table displayed is also partitioned into rows of TM states, where
      each row is an input entry of the table under a state.  To add a new
      input symbol line (a new row) to a state, press RETURN at the desired
      state.  Input symbols within a state must be unique.  To delete an input
      symbol (a row) from a state, press <CTRL>-DELETE.  If you did not load a
      table, then each state of the table is initialized to contain inputs 0
      and 1, with no other entries.

      Exit to MAIN-MENU at any time by striking the <ESC> key.

      Commenting code: In the comments area, always state the basic function
      performed by the program, and specify the correct input tape format
      and the outputs.  Comment area can also be used to draw brackets to
      identify modules/subroutines of the code, and to draw arrows to show
      flow of control.
   %!Save to disk:
      Save the current program in a permanent file.
   %!Run:
      (See "SUMMARY OF RUN SCREEN" section below.)

For initial TM input tape:
   %!Load:
      Load a tape stored on the disk.  (Always load tape AFTER loading table.)
      A directory of input tape files will appear at the lower half of the
      screen.
   %!Enter:
      Create an initial tape.  (See instructions that appear when you select
      this option.)
   %!Save:
      Save the initial tape to disk.  (The "initial tape" is the last tape
      loaded with %!Load or entered with %!Enter; it is not necessarily the
      current tape.)  A directory of input tape files will appear at the
      lower half of the screen.

%!Current State:
   Set the initial state of the Turing machine; default state is 1.
   "Initial state" is the first active state when execution begins.

%!Initialize:
   Set the current TM state to initial state, and initialize the tape to the
   last time when a %!Load or %!Enter tape has been performed (but if neither
   has been performed, then initialize the tape to all 0's).

%!Options menu:
   (See below, under "SUMMARY OF OPTIONS-MENU" heading.)

%!UTM mode:
   If the UTM mode is currently off, then toggle it on; else turn it off
   (default is off).  When UTM mode is switched on, it will have the following
   effects on TM*: the UTM program will automatically be loaded into the table;
   you will not be able to load another program; the table cannot be edited;
   the run screen will look a bit different; (see UTM help once the mode is
   on).

%!Help:
   Display the help file that you are now reading.

%!Quit:
   Quit and exit TM*.


                           SUMMARY OF OPTIONS-MENU
                           -----------------------
%!Clear/Reset options:
   Clear the Turing table and tapes currently in memory, and set all mode(s)
   to their default.  This is like quiting from TM* and starting all over
   again.  (Be sure to save your edited tables and initial tapes before doing
   this if you do not wish to lose them and they are not already saved.)

%!File management:
   For housekeeping on Turing machine files: %!Delete, %!Rename, or %!Copy
   files that have been saved on disk.

%!Generate instantaneous descriptions:
   First you will be asked, "Send output to disk? (Y/N)"; if you answer yes,
   then the state descriptions that follow will be recorded in the disk file
   named "ECHO.PRT"; otherwise the state descriptions will simply be displayed
   on the screen and not sent to a disk file.  Each state description includes:
   the current TM state number, a fixed range of the tape around the scan head,
   a count of the number of steps taken thus far, and the current tape position
   of the scan head.  You may subsequently type "PRINT ECHO.PRT" while you are
   in DOS.

%!<%!E%!S%!C%!>=exit:
   Return to MAIN-MENU.


                             SUMMARY OF RUN SCREEN
                             ---------------------
You will enter one of four different run display modes:
   (NOTE: The phrase "non-blank entries" refers to rows of the state table
    that have at least one valid entry for INP, OUT, MOV, and NST.)
   NORMAL:
      Displays four columns of TM states without comments, with a space
      between different states within a column.  This mode is first entered
      when the number of non-blank entries of the table can fit in this
      configuration.  It provides the greatest visual clarity.
   DENSE:
      This display is like NORMAL, except that there are no spaces between
      different states within a column.  This mode is first entered when the
      number of non-blank entries is too great for NORMAL, but is not too
      great for DENSE.  It shows the greatest number of non-blank entries
      that can be viewed on one screen.
   SCROLLER:
      The display shows a scrolling window just like the one found in
      %!Edit/Create in MAIN-MENU. This mode is first entered when the
      number of non-blank entries is too great for NORMAL and DENSE.  It
      is the slowest during execution.  (Lines can be added to a table
      only in this mode.)

   You may cycle through the above three displays by selecting %!Display.
   But the display(s) that cannot handle the number of non-blank entries will
   not appear.

   UTM:
      This mode is entered only when the UTM mode is turned on.  (%!HelpUTM
      calles a help file specifically for the UTM.)

There is a menu line of options at the bottom of the screen:
   %!<%!E%!S%!C%!>=Quit:
      Return to MAIN-MENU.

   %!Help: (only for NORMAL, DENSE, and SCROLLER displays)
      Shows this text that you are now reading.

   %!HelpUTM: (only for UTM display)
      Shows help specifically for UTM.  (It explains how to construct an input
      tape for the UTM, and how to run it.)

   %!Step-by-step:
      Execute one TM line at a time for each press of spacebar.

   %!Continuous:
      Execute continuously.

   H%!iSpeed:
      Execute with supressed display.  This is the fastest TM* execution
      mode, specifically built for speed.  It can perform thousands of steps,
      then pause for a command to: continue, change number of steps to
      perform, or exit.  State description will be displayed at interval steps
      along with a 1's count which shows the total number of 1's on the tape.
      (If the execution completes or execution error occurs, then simulation
      stops.)

   Edit%!Tape:
      Edit the tape with the control keys shown at the bottom of the screen.
      When execution is continued, the TM scanhead will resume at the position
      where you left it after the edit.

   %!EditTable: 
      For SCROLLER, this is the same as in %!Edit/Create in MAIN-MENU.  For
      NORMAL and DENSE, no new rows may be added.  (Not available for UTM.)


                          Switching Between Run Modes
                          ---------------------------
     When you select the %!Run option from MAIN-MENU, you enter one of four
different display modes (normal, dense, scroller, or utm), and may select
H%!ispeed for another run mode.  In addition, from the OPTIONS-MENU you may
select %!Generate instantaneous descriptions.  Basically there are only two
different run modes for TM*: when you select the %!Run option, the operation
of a TM is presented fully and graphically; but H%!iSpeed and %!Generate
instantaneous description modes only show state descriptions without animation.

     When you exit a run mode at any time, the current TM machine condition
does not change; so you can always return to that run mode or continue in a
different one.  In this way, you can switch back and forth between different
run modes any number of times.  This flexibility can be useful: after running
a "Busy5.TM" for 10,000 steps in H%!iSpeed mode, you can switch back to the
animation mode.  (Otherwise, if you wished to examine the tape in the same
state, you would have to run the machine in the graphics mode for 10,000 steps
(a long time).)


                              Limitations of TM*
                              ------------------
* The tape is not infinite; it is indexed from -5000 to 5000.  If the tape head
  moves out of this range, TM* will report an error.
* The table can contain from 1 to 99 TM states.
* Only 79 symbols or less of an initial tape can be specified.


